## SECTION 10: BUSINESS MODEL

**Base** - Free or low one-time price
- Single user, self-hosted Docker
- Capture + explain only
- No team features, no semantic search
- Target: individual developers experimenting with AI tools, small freelancers

**Plus** - Monthly subscription per workspace
- 5-50 developers
- Full feature set: capture, semantic search, governance dashboard, team management
- Self-hosted Docker (customer's infrastructure)
- Target: engineering teams at product companies, agencies, startups

**Max** - Annual contract
- Unlimited users, multi-workspace support
- Neo4j graph lineage in addition to all Plus features
- Priority support, SLA, audit export
- Can include managed hosting as an add-on
- Target: regulated industries (fintech, healthcare), large engineering organizations, companies with AI governance requirements

### Why Self-Hosted?

The primary objection to a SaaS provenance tool is data sensitivity. Provenance records contain the actual prompts developers sent and the actual code that was generated - potentially including business logic, security code, and proprietary algorithms. Self-hosted deployment eliminates this objection: data never leaves the customer's infrastructure.

This also means zero infrastructure cost for the vendor in the primary distribution model, which is favorable unit economics for a startup.

### Why Subscription Works

Provenance is not a one-time problem. As long as a team is writing AI-assisted code - which is ongoing - they need ongoing provenance capture and retention. The value compounds: the older the provenance record store, the more useful it is for audit, debugging, and governance. Teams do not churn from a tool that holds months of their AI coding history.

### Revenue Model

- Base: freemium gateway (drives awareness, converts developers who want Plus features)
- Plus: primary revenue unit (per-workspace monthly subscription)
- Max: high-ACV annual contracts with support and SLA components


